  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 319 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nMCS is tracking through north central KS early this morning and so \nfar has remained sub-severe with most wind gust reports coming in \nbetween 40-50mph. The WAA regime with modest shear (25-30 kts) \nshould be enough to maintain these storms through the overnight, but \nthink any severe gusts would be localized if they occur at all. The \nLLJ being displaced a bit to the southeast plus poor lapse rates \nwould point against a strengthening trend, and the better DCAPE is \nalso south of the sfc front, which is now draped across southern KS. \nThis main complex of storms should continue to track northeast with \nthe mean wind into southeast NE by around sunrise. A convectively \ninduced vort max still looks to hang around the area, and the HRRR \nhas been hinting at an MCV developing on the back side of the early \nmorning storms. While each run has had its differences in how robust \nany lingering storms could be, the trend has been persistent enough \nto warrant keeping low-end PoPs in the forecast through the \nafternoon hours. Subsidence behind the vort max should eventually \ntake over by late afternoon and end rain chances. Wherever storms \npersist, that could lead to even cooler temperatures than forecast, \nbut overall expect highs to be in the 80s now that we are behind the \nsfc front. Monday looks mainly dry with high pressure in the area \nand highs once again in the 80s.\n\nFor the early and especially middle part of next week, the upper \npattern shows the ridge pushed just to our south with an amplified \ntrough moving across Canada and through the Hudson Bay area. This \neventually transitions us to northwest flow aloft with embedded \nperturbations bringing additional rain chances. The NAM appears to \nbe an outlier with a complex of storms moving north to south through \ncentral KS late Monday night into early Tuesday morning. The \nforecast has low PoPs in our western zones during this time frame, \nbut have low confidence in this overall, and the instability axis is \nwest of our area as well. Late Tuesday night seems to be the next \nbest chance for storms (30-60%), mainly north of I-70. Parameters \ncould support a few severe storms, although depending on where \nstorms track we could be on the southern edge of the main threat. \nAnother wave brings another chance of storms (20-40%) Wednesday \nnight, although those details will be easier to discern once we see \nwhat setup we're left with after the previous round. \n\nTemperatures are forecast in the 80s to low 90s for the second half \nof the week, but as has been iterated in previous forecasts, the \nrange widens in the ensembles owing to uncertainty related to timing \nand placement of storms, as well as a frontal boundary nearby. The \nupper ridge to our southwest builds by the end of the week, which \nmay lead to a warming trend into next weekend.",
